<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Premier League</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Premier League is the top division of English men's football, founded in 1992 when the leading clubs broke away from the Football League. Backed by large television contracts, it became the most watched and richest football league in the world, and its global audience has made it a major British cultural export.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/premier-league.jpg" alt="Premier League" width="330" height="512" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Espandero · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trophée_Premier_League_13-14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Despite major European success in the 1970s and early 1980s, the mid and late 1980s were a low point for English football. Stadiums were ageing with poor facilities, hooliganism was rife, and after the Heysel Stadium disaster of 1985 all English clubs faced a five-year ban from European competition, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> given an extra year. The Football League First Division, the top level since 1888, lagged behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s Serie A and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s La Liga in attendance and revenue, and several top English players had moved abroad.</p><p>At the close of the 1990-91 season a proposal was tabled for a new league that would bring more money into the game. The Founder Members Agreement, signed on 17 July 1991 by the top-flight clubs, set the principles of the FA Premier League, which was founded on 20 February 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The league began with 22 clubs, a number the top flight had reached only at the start of 1991-92, and was reduced to 20 in 1995, when four teams were relegated and only two promoted. In the 2000s Arsenal, Chelsea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and Manchester United dominated and were called the Big Four; from 1999-2000 to 2008-09 three of them finished in the top four every season. Manchester United won six titles in the decade, Arsenal two and Chelsea two under José Mourinho. Arsenal's unbeaten 2003-04 season earned them the nickname the Invincibles, the only such season of the Premier League era.</p><p>After 2009 Tottenham Hotspur and Manchester City regularly reached the top four, forming what came to be called the Big Six. Manchester City's title in 2011-12 was the first by a club outside the Big Four since Blackburn Rovers in 1994-95. Video assistant referees were introduced from the 2019-20 season, the season in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> won their first Premier League title, ending a 30-year wait for a top-flight championship. In October 2020 Manchester United and Liverpool announced Project Big Picture, a proposal for closer alignment between top clubs and the English Football League, which the league's leadership and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> for Culture, Media and Sport criticised.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Football Association Premier League Limited is a corporation owned by its member clubs, each a shareholder with one vote on rule changes and contracts. The clubs choose a chairman, a chief executive and a board. The Football Association is not involved in daily operations but holds a veto as a special shareholder over the choice of chairman and chief executive and over new rules. Each club plays the others twice, home and away, for 38 games, with three points for a win and one for a draw, and the lowest-placed teams are relegated to the EFL Championship.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Premier League” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British popular culture, from the BBC, founded in 1922, to the Beatles, James Bond and the <b>Premier League</b>, reached a global audience, and English became the world's chief language of international communication, a position reinforced by both British and American influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
